5d/5d8f57afa16e99c13e6e36c5544a7c7594da13321bd3d01692a5a296a20953d7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425929 1072 #uuid "1654ded7-c0bc-579c-a9e9-6daa6a1243e7" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425929 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/ce/ce9b9f3435a94b3f9f87b195e5b1591c80281b23cfb76ceb8e79cdeec3ae66b0.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425928 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425930 1069 "q-80526" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425930 1071 "![](images/88/8866056ce5c7c80ed8452246cc3a40074b6841615ed6ef472e7a1612709e51fa.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,1]\\times [-3,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425930 1072 #uuid "19ad1761-3136-5be8-b038-5aa62265f48b" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425930 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425927 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425931 1069 "q-129893" 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534566 17592186045417 true] [17592186425931 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$